karr #35 split _struct_to_object_expanded() out of struct_to_object() and
established the rule, stated in the comment on lib/IO/K8s.pm:757-760:

  "Callers that already hold a resolved class name must use
   _struct_to_object_expanded() instead"

new_object(), json_to_object() and _inflate_struct() were converted. Five
call sites in the apiextensions union classes were not, and they are
exactly the case the rule describes -- they pass a hard-coded, fully
qualified class name:

  lib/IO/K8s/ApiextensionsApiserver/Pkg/Apis/Apiextensions/V1/
    JSON.pm:19
    JSONSchemaPropsOrArray.pm:84, :87
    JSONSchemaPropsOrBool.pm:87
    JSONSchemaPropsOrStringArray.pm:88

All five pass $PROPS =
IO::K8s::ApiextensionsApiserver::Pkg::Apis::Apiextensions::V1::JSONSchemaProps
(or the literal), so they hit expand_class()'s second line --
`return $class if $class =~ /^IO::K8s::/`  -- and come straight back out.
No bug today; verified by measurement, the full suite is green either way.

Why it is still worth closing: this is the re-expansion karr #35 removed,
left behind in the one place that was not on the karr #35 call path. It
survives only because that early return sits above everything else in
expand_class(). A future reordering of the search order -- and this file
has been reordered twice now, karr #34 and karr #35 -- puts a second full
lookup back on the FROM_STRUCT path for the union types, which is the
hardest path in the distribution to reason about.

Mechanical change: s/struct_to_object/_struct_to_object_expanded/ at the
five sites. Test coverage already exists (t/31_apiextensions_unions.t);
the change should not need new assertions, which is itself the check --
if t/31 moves, the substitution was not what it looked like.

